
<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You already know that <code>su</code> requires a password for the target user.<br><code>sudo</code> is a great tool for elevated privileges, but it&#8217;s heavy sometimes.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">What would you use in a small, flexible environment?</p>



<span id="more-9553"></span>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><code>doas</code> is a smaller, lightweight tool for the same task.<br>Originally developed for OpenBSD, then <code>opendoas</code> is available on other BSD and Linux systems.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><code>doas</code> is small and easier to configure than <code>sudo</code>.<br>In exchange it doesn&#8217;t allow such a granular configuration and audit setup.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yet in small environments <code>doas</code> is a viable competitor to <code>sudo</code>.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Smaller code base doesn&#8217;t automatically mean more secure.<br>But it can mean smaller exposure, of course.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Ansible supports <code>doas</code> as a privilege escalation method through its <code>become</code> system.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The choice is yours.<br></p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Install Doas</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">On Debian and compatible systems it&#8217;s part of the main repository.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can install <code>doas</code> with <code>apt</code> as the root user:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">su -</div><div class="cm-line">apt update</div><div class="cm-line">apt install opendoas</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Configure Doas</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you use <code>sudo</code> for a couple of users or groups, <code>doas</code> will shine for you.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The configuration is managed in a single <code>/etc/doas.conf</code> file that you create.<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">touch /etc/doas.conf</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The configuration file format is extremely simple.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Create a rule for your <code>localadmins</code> group:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">permit :localadmins as root</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Do you want to keep the authentication valid for some time?<br></p>



<pre class="wp-block-preformatted">permit persist :localadmins as root
</pre>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Depending on how OpenDoas was built, persistence support may not be available.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you need passwordless <code>doas</code> for a specific user called <code>joe</code>:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">permit nopass joe as root</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">OpenDoas supports checking the configuration:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">doas -C /etc/doas.conf</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">It&#8217;s this simple.<br></p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Harden The Configuration</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Do not let the configuration file in that state.<br>Other users may read its content.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Make it only readable by the <code>root</code> user.<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">chown root:root /etc/doas.conf</div><div class="cm-line">chmod 0400 /etc/doas.conf</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Other users won&#8217;t be able to read its contents.<br></p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">You can see <code>doas</code> is very similar to <code>sudo</code>.<br>You set it up, and you let it work.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Basic audit logging is available through syslog.<br>On systems using journald, you can also inspect the journal.<br>It can be disabled by the <code>nolog</code> option in the configuration file.<br></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If you use BASH, and you installed <code>bash-completion</code>, add it to your <code>.bashrc</code>:<br></p>


<div class="wp-block-code">
	<div class="cm-editor">
		<div class="cm-scroller">
			
<pre>
<code><div class="cm-line">complete -F _command doas</div><div class="cm-line"></div></code></pre>
		</div>
	</div>
</div>


<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><code>doas</code> is a nice solution if <code>sudo</code> is too heavy for your environment.<br></p>



